From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:25:20 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: apple: aop_spkr: add the J616s headphone jack streams

The J616s 3.5 mm jack is two more services of the AOP firmware that
runs the speakers: "cout" drives MCA cluster 2 (serializer ms02, mck2
from the 3.072 MHz NCO: a 64-bit frame at 48 kHz with two 32-bit slots,
24 valid bits) fed by base-ns ADMAC channel 4 (TX2), and "cin " brings
the headset microphone back on the same serializer through channel 5
(RX2). These are the iop-audio-controller streams 'tuoc' and ' nic' of
the machine's device tree. The firmware arbitrates the MCA and the
audio power states between the speaker and jack streams.

Each jack direction is a service of its own, with its own attachment,
users and power states, like the speaker's: back-end startup raises
pw1, prepare requests pwrd (output flag 2 for "cout", input flag 1 for
"cin ", as the microphones use), hw_free drops back to pw1. Neither has
a sense ring, so the service's ring becomes optional, and a jack
service's prepare only raises pwrd when its serializer is not running
yet. The jack's 8- and 4-byte frames need whole 16-byte ADMAC bursts
per period, so its front-ends constrain the period size to them.

The jack is a second component, "t6040-aop-jack", for a sound card of
its own: front-end j616-jack-pcm-0 (playback on "cout", 2 channels,
capture on "cin", 1 channel, S24_LE at 48 kHz), j616-jack-pcm-1 (only
the stream widget macaudio's fixed routes need; it refuses to open) and
the "jack" back-end. A component binds to one card only, and a card
binds only once all of its codecs have probed: on its own card, a jack
codec that never answers costs the jack, not the speakers. Both
components sit on the audio node, and ASoC resolves a sound-dai there
by asking each component in turn. The jack component answers argument
4 through of_xlate_dai_name and defers every other argument, and it
registers before the speaker component, so neither card can probe into
a half-registered state. A jack registration failure is logged and
leaves the speakers alone. The jack component is registered when the
audio node lists the "cout" and "cin" DMA channels.

System sleep takes both jack services down with the streams suspended,
as for the speaker: suspend drops a running service to pw1 and notes
that it ran, resume brings it back to pwrd before ASoC unmutes the
codec. Both services are resumed even if the first fails; the first
error is returned.

